Lab 3 Answers
=============

1. My model was registered as version 1. A run artifact belongs to one training
   run, while a registered model has a shared name, versions, and aliases.

2. Aliases like `champion` and `challenger` replaced the old stages. Versions
   keep model history, and an alias can easily point to a newer version.

3. The MLflow URI avoids hard-coding a local `.pth` path. To use a newer model,
   I only need to move the `champion` alias and restart the API.

4. Copying dependency files first lets Docker reuse the dependency cache. If I
   only edit `serve.py`, Docker rebuilds the source layer instead of reinstalling
   everything.

5. My final image was about 2.06 GB. PyTorch and the Python dependencies were
   the largest layers. The multi-stage build keeps builder tools out of the
   final image.

6. Without `.dockerignore`, Docker sends unnecessary files, making builds slower
   and possibly larger. A Windows `.venv` could also break the Linux image.

7. Inside the container, `127.0.0.1` means the container itself.
   `host.docker.internal` points back to the Windows host.

8. Yes. A new container can use the same image because the code and dependencies
   are already inside it. The model is loaded from MLflow when the container
   starts.

9. The image still needs a versioned tag and must be pushed to a container
   registry so another machine can pull the exact same image.
